course: everyday
lesson: 62
title: Dogs and rabbits in your care
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Looking after a dog or a rabbit means knowing what its body needs and what can hurt it, and telling an adult quickly when something is wrong.
explain: A dog is strong and fast: a big dog can pull a child over or into a road, and a dog that runs off can be hit by a car, so walks and time off the lead need care.
explain: A rabbit is a gentle animal with a fragile back and teeth that never stop growing; it needs hay to chew, space to hop and a rabbit friend to live with.
explain: Animals show us how they feel: a rabbit that thumps its back foot is warning of danger, and a rabbit that leaps and twists in the air, called a binky, is happy.
explain: A boy in Wellington hears thunder and finds his dog shaking under the bed. He does not drag it out; he stays calm nearby, shuts the doors and windows and lets it hide until the storm passes.
explain: A family in Florida walked their dog at midday on hot pavement; its paw pads were burnt and blistered, and it limped for weeks.
explain: A girl in Wales kept her rabbit alone in a small hutch in the sun with no shade; on a hot day the rabbit got heatstroke and died.
fact: A child walks a big or strong dog only with an adult, because it can pull a child over or into a road.
fact: A dog is let off the lead only where it is allowed and only if it comes back when called.
fact: Sticks can splinter and stab a dog's mouth or throat, so a ball or rubber toy is safer to throw.
fact: Loud noises like thunder and fireworks (on New Year's Eve or Bonfire Night in Britain) frighten many dogs, and a scared dog may run away.
fact: A dog in pain may bite even its own family, so an adult must handle an ill or injured dog.
fact: Gentle brushing in the direction the fur grows removes loose hair and lets you check for ticks and lumps; a tick is a tiny blood-sucking bug that clings to the skin and feels like a small round lump.
fact: A dog that eats medicine, a sock or other wrong things may need a vet, so you tell an adult straight away.
fact: You hold a rabbit by supporting its bottom and back legs and keep it low, because a fall or a kick can break its back.
fact: Rabbits are social animals that need a rabbit friend and lots of space to hop and run.
fact: A rabbit should eat mostly hay with some fresh greens; carrots are sugary and only a treat.
fact: A rabbit thumps its back foot as a warning that it senses danger.
fact: A happy rabbit jumps and twists in the air; this is called a binky.
fact: Rabbit teeth keep growing all their lives and are worn down by chewing hay.
fact: A rabbit that stops eating or stops doing droppings may have an emergency gut problem.
fact: Rabbits get heatstroke easily and can die in hot sun without shade and water.
fact: Pavement in hot sun can be hot enough to burn a dog's paws.
exercise: Your neighbour asks if you would like to walk her big, strong dog on your own. What is the safe answer? | Only walk it together with an adult | Yes, if you hold the lead very tightly | Yes, if you only go along the main road | Only walk it when it is dark and quiet
exercise: When is it fine to let a dog off its lead? | Where it is allowed and when it comes back when called | Whenever the dog pulls hard on the lead | Near a busy road, so it can stretch its legs and burn off energy | In a field of sheep, so it can run
exercise: Why is a rubber ball safer to throw for a dog than a stick? | Sticks can splinter and stab its mouth or throat | Sticks are much too heavy for most dogs to carry back | Dogs cannot see sticks on the grass | Balls make dogs run more slowly
exercise: During fireworks your dog shakes and hides behind the sofa. What should you do? | Stay calm nearby, let it hide and keep doors shut | Pull it out and take it outside to watch, so it learns not to be scared | Tell it off for being silly | Open the door so it can run off its fear
exercise: You find your dog limping and yelping in the garden. What should you do? | Call an adult and not touch it yourself | Pick it up and carry it inside the house | Press on the sore leg to find the problem | Give it some of your own medicine to help
exercise: Which way should you brush a dog's coat? | Gently in the direction the fur grows | Hard against the way the fur grows | In little circles all over | Only on its tail and ears
exercise: Your dog has just eaten a sock. What should you do? | Tell an adult straight away | Wait to see if it comes out by itself | Give the dog lots of water and forget it | Hide the other sock so no one finds out
exercise: How should you hold a rabbit? | Support its bottom and back legs and keep it low | Lift it up by its ears | Hold it under its front legs only, letting its back legs dangle | Hold it high so it can see around
exercise: What does a rabbit need to be happy? | A rabbit friend and lots of space to hop and run | A small hutch all to itself | A cage in a dark, quiet shed, away from noise and people | Only lots of carrots each day
exercise: What should most of a rabbit's food be? | Hay | Carrots | Lettuce | Bread
exercise: Your rabbit suddenly thumps its back foot on the floor. What is it telling you? | It senses danger and is giving a warning | It is happy and wants to play with you | It is hungry and wants carrots | It is tired and wants to sleep
exercise: Your rabbit leaps into the air and twists its body. What is this? | A happy jump called a binky | A sign that it is in pain | A warning that it is angry | A sign that it is too hot
exercise: Why do rabbits need to chew lots of hay? | Their teeth never stop growing and hay wears them down | Hay makes their fur shiny | Hay stops them from thumping | Hay helps them sleep through the night without waking up
exercise: Your rabbit has not eaten or done any droppings since yesterday. What should you do? | Tell an adult at once, it can be an emergency | Wait a few more days to see | Give it lots of extra carrots to tempt it to eat again | Put it in the sun to warm it up
exercise: Your rabbit's front teeth look much too long. What should you do? | Tell an adult so a vet can check them | Cut them yourself with scissors | Give it softer food so it eats less | Nothing, long teeth are healthy for rabbits
test: A girl in Edinburgh asks why carrots should be only a treat for her rabbit. What is the reason? | Carrots are sugary; hay keeps its teeth and gut healthy | Carrots are poisonous for rabbits | Rabbits cannot bite through carrots | Carrots make rabbits thump their feet and get cross with each other
test: A boy in Dallas lets his dog off the lead in the park, but the dog never comes when called. What could happen? | It could run off and be hit by a car | Nothing, dogs always find their way back | It will soon learn to come back by itself | It will stay close because it is free
test: Why might a gentle family dog bite its owner after being hit by a bike? | A dog in pain may bite even people it loves | It thinks the owner hit it with the bike | Dogs always bite after a shock | It wants to make the owner go away forever
test: A family in Sydney plans to watch the New Year fireworks. What is the best plan for their nervous dog? | Keep it indoors with doors and windows shut and someone calm nearby | Take it along to the fireworks so it gets used to the bangs and stops being frightened | Leave it tied up alone in the yard | Let it run loose in the garden all evening
test: While brushing his dog, a boy in Vermont feels a small round lump stuck to its skin. Why is it good that he noticed? | It may be a tick, and brushing helps find ticks and lumps | Brushing makes lumps go away by itself | Lumps mean the dog needs a lot more brushing every day | It shows the brush is too hard
test: A child picks up a rabbit, it kicks hard and falls to the floor. Why is this so dangerous? | A rabbit can break its back if it falls or kicks out | Rabbits cannot land on their feet at all, unlike cats | The rabbit will run away and hide | The rabbit's ears can get hurt
test: A rabbit lives alone in a tiny hutch at the bottom of the garden. What is wrong with this? | Rabbits are social and need a friend and space to run | Nothing, rabbits like to be alone | Rabbits should live inside a cupboard | It should have two hutches to choose from, one for sleeping and one for eating
test: Which of these is a sign that a pet rabbit is happy? | It jumps and twists in the air | It thumps its back foot on the floor | It suddenly stops eating its hay | It hides away and will not come out
test: Which is true about rabbit teeth? | They keep growing and need chewing to wear them down | They stop growing when the rabbit is one year old | They fall out and grow back every year | They are only used for eating carrots
test: can i take the big dog out by myself, he pulls heaps | No, walk a strong dog only with an adult | Yes, just hold the lead tighter with both hands | Yes, if it is a short walk | Yes, if you go near the road
test: threw a stick for the dog, she loves it | A ball or rubber toy is safer, sticks splinter | Sticks are the best and safest toy | Any stick is fine as long as it is big and thick | Sticks are safe if they are wet
test: bunny keeps thumping her back foot, wot does that mean | She senses danger and is warning | She is very happy and playing | She wants a carrot right now | She is trying to dig a hole
test: rabbit hasnt eaten or pooped since yday | Tell an adult, it may be a gut emergency | It is fine, rabbits often skip days | Give it lots of carrots to cheer up | Wait a week and see what happens, it'll probably sort itself out
test: it's super hot, the bunny's hutch is in full sun | Move it into shade and give fresh water now | Leave it, rabbits like heat | Cover the hutch with a black sheet to keep the sun off | Give the rabbit a hot bath
test: wanna walk the dog at noon, pavement's boiling | Wait till it is cooler or walk on grass | Go now, dogs have tough feet, they don't feel it | Go but walk faster | Put socks on the dog and go
test: Lily says rabbits are happy alone in a little hutch. right? | No, they need space and usually a rabbit friend | Yes, rabbits like small spaces where they feel safe | Yes, they sleep all day | Yes, unless it is cold
test: heard a dog choked on a stick at the park and the vet had to operate, it was so bad | Real lesson: sticks can splinter and stab a dog's mouth, so throw a ball | Real lesson: dogs should never be allowed to play at parks without a muzzle on | It proves vets operate on every dog | It is gossip, so sticks are fine
test: my mate says u can let any dog off the lead in the park, they always come back | Wrong; let a dog off only where allowed and if it comes back when called | He is right, all dogs come back | He is right, if the park is big | He is right, all dogs come back once they've had a good run and a bark at the ducks
test: the Patels' dog ran off on bonfire night, people say they're useless owners, pass it on | Do not pass it on; fireworks frighten many dogs and it could happen to anyone | Pass it on, because they lost it | Pass it on, so they buy a lead | Pass it on, and say it was found hurt by the road and they did not even go and look for it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the proper manner of holding a rabbit? | Supporting its hindquarters and hind legs, the rabbit being kept low | Lifting it by the ears | Holding it high above the head | Holding it by the scruff of the neck with one hand, as one would a kitten
exam: Identify the arrangement under which a child may walk a large or strong dog. | Only in the company of an adult | Alone, provided the lead is long | Alone, provided the dog is old and slow | Only along busy roads with lots of traffic
exam: It being given that a dog has swallowed a sock, select the appropriate course of action. | To inform an adult straight away, as a veterinary surgeon may be required | To wait until it passes naturally | To feed the dog a large meal | To pull it out through the mouth oneself, however far down the sock may have gone
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning a dog in pain. | It may bite even its own family, so an adult should handle it | It will remain gentle with children | It should be hugged closely in order to comfort it and calm it down | It should be lifted by the child
